| Paragraph / section | Word count | Fiduciary-intensity score | Duty-of-loyalty flags | Loyalty-risk density |
|---|---:|---:|---|---:|
| §1 Approval | 86 | High | Related-party benefit; no recusal stated | 0.09 points/word |
| §2 Process | 124 | Medium | Disclosure language, but independence unclear | 0.04 points/word |
| §3 Ratification | 41 | Very high | Conflict waiver / interested-director approval | 0.17 points/word |

For this purpose, I would treat **fiduciary intensity** as the degree to which a paragraph exercises or constrains board discretion over corporate assets, transactions, conflicts, compensation, disclosures, approvals, or delegations.

The **duty-of-loyalty exposure per word** would be a normalized score based chiefly on language involving:

- director/officer or affiliate benefits;
- related-party transactions;
- compensation, equity, loans, indemnification, or waivers;
- conflict disclosures and recusals;
- independent-director or disinterested-stockholder approval;
- fairness / arm’s-length findings;
- ratification of prior conduct;
- vague authorization to negotiate or approve terms.

A short provision approving an insider transaction without disclosure, recusal, or fairness findings will often have the highest exposure density—even if a longer section contains more total fiduciary content.

