Do not compare those quantities directly. A phonon measurement is not “solvent,” and a rival group’s publication curve is not a calibration standard. In the proposal, separate the case into three linked benchmarks:

| Question | Appropriate benchmark | Useful metrics |
|---|---|---|
| Will the cryostat improve the science? | Current setup, reference data, and science-case requirements | Sample-temperature stability, accessible \(Q\)-space, background, vibration, equilibration time, successful maps per beamtime day, uncertainty in phonon energies/linewidths |
| Can the lab sustain it? | Five- to seven-year total-cost-of-ownership model | Purchase/install cost, service, cryogens/power, technician time, facility integration, downtime reserve, cost per usable experiment hour |
| Will it improve competitive output? | Matched peer cohort, normalized for group size and access | Three-year rolling phonon-dispersion papers per active FTE or allocated beam day; lead-author papers; field-normalized citations; time from measurement to publication |

### 1. Benchmark the actual measurement bottleneck

Start with 2–4 flagship science cases and specify what the new cryostat must enable that the present system cannot. For phonon-dispersion work, that commonly means:

- temperature range and *sample* temperature stability, not merely cold-head temperature;
- thermal equilibration time and reproducibility after temperature changes;
- scattering/optical-window background;
- reciprocal-space access and shadowing from the cryostat geometry;
- vibration-induced broadening or alignment drift;
- sample-exchange and cooldown time;
- compatibility with the relevant IXS, INS, Raman, or Brillouin setup.

Use an identical reference sample and measurement geometry, where possible, to compare the incumbent configuration with vendor or facility evidence for the proposed system. Report data-level outcomes such as:

- phonon-energy and linewidth uncertainty;
- signal-to-background ratio;
- fraction of planned \(Q\)-points obtained at acceptance-quality signal;
- complete dispersion maps per allocated beamtime day;
- fraction of runs lost to thermal instability, failed cooldowns, or geometry restrictions.

A useful throughput model is:

\[
N_{\rm maps/yr} =
\frac{H_{\rm scheduled}\, U_{\rm instrument}\, f_{\rm usable}}
{t_{\rm load}+t_{\rm cool}+t_{\rm align}+t_{\rm map}}
\times f_{\rm successful}
\]

Estimate every term from your own recent experiment logs, not vendor specifications alone.

Importantly, do not claim that the cryostat improves intrinsic energy resolution unless current resolution is demonstrably limited by vibration, background, or thermal instability. In many IXS/INS experiments, the spectrometer or beamline—not the cryostat—sets the nominal energy resolution.

### 2. Treat “solvency” as lifecycle sustainability

Build a conservative total-cost-of-ownership model over the expected instrument life:

\[
{\rm TCO} =
C_{\rm purchase}+C_{\rm installation}+C_{\rm interface}
+\sum_{t=1}^{T}
(C_{\rm service}+C_{\rm cryogens/power}+C_{\rm staff}+C_{\rm consumables})
\]

Include facility-specific costs: safety approval, transport, window replacements, compressor maintenance, integration engineering, and a downtime/repair contingency.

Then report:

- annual operating cost;
- cost per usable experiment hour;
- incremental cost per acceptance-quality dispersion map;
- cost relative to alternatives: maintaining the old unit, renting/accessing a facility cryostat, sharing with a collaborator, or purchasing a lower-specification system.

Avoid treating future publications as direct cash income. For a grant, it is more credible to show that operating funds, technical support, and service coverage are committed under a conservative utilization scenario.

### 3. Use peer publication curves only as a normalized sanity check

Construct a peer cohort of groups that are genuinely comparable: same scattering/spectroscopy method, comparable materials portfolio, similar access to major facilities, and roughly similar team size. Do not compare against broad departmental or consortium publication totals.

For each group, count only papers in which phonon-dispersion data are central to the result. Plot a trailing three-year yield curve such as:

\[
Y(t)=\frac{\text{phonon-dispersion lead papers in prior 3 years}}
{\text{active research FTE-years}}
\]

Where data are available, also normalize by awarded beamtime or facility access. Report the peer median and interquartile range, rather than a simplistic rank.

Account for publication lag: a cryostat acquired this year may affect papers one to several years later. If publicly available acquisition dates can be identified, an event-time plot around equipment installation can be descriptive, but do not present it as causal proof; staffing, funding, sample pipelines, and facility access are major confounders.

### 4. Use a clear decision rule

The purchase is well supported if, in a conservative scenario:

1. it enables one or more high-value measurements unavailable with the present setup;
2. it materially increases usable data throughput or reduces failed runs;
3. the lab has committed operating support for its full lifecycle;
4. the relevant facility confirms mechanical, safety, and beamline compatibility;
5. the projected output moves the group toward the matched-peer range without relying on implausible assumptions.

Conversely, do not buy it if the real bottleneck is allocated beamtime, sample growth, detector resolution, analysis staffing, or publication pipeline rather than cryogenic capability.

A grant-ready framing would be:

> We will evaluate the proposed cryostat against the current environment using identical reference samples and beamline conditions, with performance assessed by sample-temperature stability, reciprocal-space access, background, map-completion rate, and reproducibility of extracted phonon energies and linewidths. A seven-year lifecycle model will demonstrate sustainable operation, including service, cryogenic utilities, staffing, and facility-integration costs. Competitive positioning will be assessed using a matched cohort of phonon-spectroscopy groups, normalized by active research capacity and accounting for publication lag. This approach distinguishes scientific capability, operational sustainability, and research output rather than inferring instrument value from raw publication counts.
